WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.258 --> 00:00:06.590
EMIL KINER: Hi.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.119
Thank you for joining us today.

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.880
Today, we'll be telling
you about what's

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.203
new in network security
on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.203 --> 00:00:13.120
My name is Emil Kiner.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.880
I'm a product manager on
the Cloud Networking team

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.610
and I'm joined by my
colleague, Tracy Jiang, also

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.702
product manager on the team.

00:00:20.702 --> 00:00:22.410
First, we're going to
start by giving you

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.350
a brief overview of network
security on Google Cloud.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.437
From there, I'll
dive into Cloud Armor

00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:29.520
and share all the latest
and greatest developments

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.700
in the product.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.410
Next, Tracy will take
over and talk about all

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.480
of the new things
with Cloud Firewall,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.330
and also share about
our new product offering

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:43.290
around Packet Mirroring.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.480
Security follows a shared
responsibility model

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.800
on Google Cloud.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.990
We work to secure our
network and infrastructure

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.630
and provide customers the
tools necessary to do so as

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.620
well in their own projects.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.260
We enable and
encourage customers

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.420
to follow a defense
in depth approach,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.270
and deploy security controls at
various levels of their stack

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.970
and infrastructure to
enforce access controls

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.430
and ensure data and mission
critical workloads remain

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.470
private and secure.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.670
On Google Cloud, we have several
key network security controls.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.340
Starting from the outside
in we have Cloud Armor,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.950
the Identity-Aware Proxy,
and the global load balancers

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.040
as our layer seven controls
at the edge of the network.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.260
Cloud Armor, in conjunction
with the global LVs

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.200
protect applications
from DDoS attacks.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.990
The IAP is part of our
BeyondCorp line of products

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.020
to allow access to internal
applications and workloads

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.910
without needing a VPN.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.970
Firewalls, in red,
apply at the VPC level,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.360
and can be used to customize
access for north-south traffic

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.470
as well as the east-west traffic
into, out of, or through a VPC.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.720
Firewalls are used to
define VPC boundaries

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.320
but can be as
granular as per host.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.810
VPC Service Controls
in blue can be

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.620
used to control access
to and from GCP managed

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.150
API services, such as BigQuery
or Google Cloud Storage

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.040
to or from the internet,
into or out of your VPCs,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.760
or on premise resources.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.970
In yellow we have our latest
offering, Packet Mirroring,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.180
that allows you to deploy a TAP
and mirror packets at the VM

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.090
or container cluster boundary
to a configurable collector

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.920
destination.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.680
Finally, we also support
sending outbound traffic

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.070
through cloud net to enhance
privacy and security, as well

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:28.440
as various interconnect and
VPN configuration options.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.510
With Cloud Armor, customers
are able to protect

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.700
their applications
from DDos attacks,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.820
filter incoming
requests by geography,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.690
or most Layer 7 parameters like
request headers or cookies.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.360
Moreover as a web
application firewall,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.280
Cloud Armor contains
pre-configured rules

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.580
to prevent and mitigate
the most common attacks

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.740
and vulnerability
exploit attempts.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.750
Users have access to
real-time telemetry

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.170
in the form of Cloud Logs
containing Cloud Armor's

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.530
decisions on a per request
basis, as well as a real time

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.710
monitoring dashboard that gives
granular views of allowed,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.075
denied, and previewed traffic.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.770
With Google Cloud Armor,
customers' applications and web

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.640
sites are protected with
an unrivaled level of DDoS

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.250
protection as well
as a web application

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.790
firewall able to satisfy
internal security policies as

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.730
well as external
regulatory regimes.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.860
The DDoS protection
Cloud customers receive

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.410
is the same that has been
developed over the past 20

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.680
years to help protect and ensure
the availability of Google's

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.660
own services.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.480
In conjunction with the global
load balancing infrastructure,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.430
we are able to
absorb and mitigate

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.170
Layer 3 or Layer 4
network or volumetric DDoS

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.510
attacks with no impact
on customer workloads.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.240
This mitigation
happens automatically

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.580
far upstream from
customer infrastructure

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.740
across all types of
global load balancers,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.160
including the HTTPS load
balancer, SSL proxy,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.180
and TCP proxy.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.730
Users can then configure
Cloud Armor security policies

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.610
to enforce Layer 7
filtering policies,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.880
and WAF rules on a per
back-end service basis.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.380
Cloud Armor security
policies can

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.010
be used to enforce IP or
geobased access controls,

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.490
mitigate OWASP Top 10 risks,
and otherwise filter traffic

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:17.320
upstream of an application
based on layer 7 attributes.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.990
Today, we are proud to
share that a rich set of WAF

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:23.220
capabilities for Cloud Armor
is generally available.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.760
Pre-configured WAF rules can
help mitigate the OWASP Top 10

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:27.477
risks.

00:04:27.477 --> 00:04:29.310
We have ported over the
[INAUDIBLE] security

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.510
core rule set, introducing
rules to detect and block

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.770
SQL injection, as well as
cross site scripting attacks.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.860
Additionally, rules for local
file inclusion, remote file

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.720
inclusion, and
remote code execution

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.352
are available as beta.

00:04:44.352 --> 00:04:46.560
The rest of the core rule
set is targeted for release

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.810
over the course of this year.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.170
With geo-based rules, customers
can enforce access controls

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.110
based on the source
geography of each request.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.380
IP to geomappings are sourced
from Google's geo-team,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.800
so you can be sure
of its accuracy.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.130
We've also launched an
extensible rules language

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.130
that allows users to configure
a custom Layer 7 filtering

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:12.770
policies across request headers,
request parameters, or cookies.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.080
Finally, Cloud Armor is now
integrated with the Cloud

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.200
Security Command Center with
Cloud Armor findings being

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.350
sent to the CSCC dashboard
to alert defenders

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.600
of potential attacks against
protected applications

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.100
or websites.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.060
We have recently
greatly increased

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.010
the flexibility of a
Cloud Armor deployment

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.010
to protect an expanded set
of customer infrastructure

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:38.150
on GCP, as well as in hybrid
use cases located on prem,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.560
or in other cloud providers.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.130
Cloud Armor now supports
protecting Cloud CDN origin

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.620
servers by enforcing
Cloud Armor security

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.910
policies on dynamic requests,
as well as cache misses destined

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.400
for the CDN origin server.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.830
Customer applications
are often complex,

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.440
serving both cacheable
static content, as well as

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.200
dynamic requests from
the same services.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.360
With Cloud Armor able
to inspect and filter

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.460
requests destined to
CDN origin servers,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.970
users can now mitigate
computationally expensive

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.130
cache-busting
attacks, as well as

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.920
help protect dynamic portions
of websites and applications

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.920
from the OWASP Top 10 risks.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.680
Enterprises often need to
enforce a consistent set

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.790
of security controls
to their applications,

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:25.030
no matter where they are
deployed, whether they

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:28.810
are migrating to GCP or
running in permanently hybrid

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.250
configurations.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.430
We've recently launched support
for internet network endpoint

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.550
groups that allow customers to
leverage all of Google's Edge

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.610
infrastructure, including our
global load balancers, cloud

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:43.550
CDN, and Cloud Armor to protect
their website or applications,

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.280
no matter where they are hosted.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.420
Now Google can help
protect your applications,

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.110
whether from DDoS attacks
or other common web

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.270
attacks without customers
having to deploy

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.710
their infrastructure on GCP.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.400
Finally, GKE ingress
support for Cloud Armor has

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.500
reached general availability.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.130
GKE users can help protect
their containerized workloads

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.370
by placing them behind
Google's global load balancers,

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.160
and configuring Cloud Armor
security policies for layer 7

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.290
filtering and WAF use cases.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.140
With that, I'd like to
hand it off to Tracy

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.220
to discuss Cloud Firewalls.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.060
TRACY JIANG: Thank you, Emil.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:24.230
In the next section, we're going
to be going through the Cloud

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.830
Firewalls.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.635
First, we'll give you a quick
introduction about the DPC file

00:07:29.635 --> 00:07:30.360
rules.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.940
Then we're going to be
talking about two new modules

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:35.460
that we recently brought
to market to strengthen

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.320
our firewall offerings.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.840
Google Cloud Firewall has
a different architecture

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.910
than the traditional firewalls.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.580
In a sense, it does
not have a physical box

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.770
that you need to configure
and pipe your traffic

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.420
through for filtering.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:53.330
Instead it is offered
as a virtual firewall

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:58.170
service that is fully integrated
into Google SDN fabric.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.520
The policy enforcement
happens on the host

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.430
for each individual
[INAUDIBLE] instances.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.320
Compared with a
traditional model,

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.710
this type of architecture will
give you higher scalability

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.420
and has no choke point.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:17.050
It is a fully scalable
and stateful firewall

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:20.620
that inspects both ingress
and egress traffic.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:24.760
Users can also make use of the
network TAC service account

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.180
to define the fine
granular firewall rules,

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.800
and implement their micro
segmentation strategies.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:38.635
As we know, the VPC Firewall
operates at the VPC level.

00:08:38.635 --> 00:08:41.859
It allows users to define an
independent set of firewall

00:08:41.859 --> 00:08:45.370
rules for each individual VPC.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.010
This gives you,
the project owners,

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.740
a lot of the independence
and flexibility

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.530
in controlling your
firewall rule set,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:57.440
and operate in such a
dynamic cloud environment.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.100
However, it does
present some challenges

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.000
for corporate security
teams in large enterprises.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.890
This type of
organizations typically

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:08.620
has hundreds, sometimes
even thousands

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:12.310
of projects and networks
in their same organization.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:16.360
Often times, they want to
implement some common rules

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:20.540
across all the networks
and the projects.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.330
Without a central control,
it's typically very difficult

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.480
to do so.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.610
Also the project
owner oftentimes

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:30.940
do not have an [INAUDIBLE]
depth of security knowledge

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.710
as their security team does.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.960
Therefore, they could
make some mistakes,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.350
and misconfigure
their firewall rules,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.490
and then expose their
workload at risk.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.810
How to maintain a dynamic
and safe cloud environment,

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:49.880
and strike a delicate balance
between control and dedication

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.110
has been the constant battle for
those central security teams.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.810
Hierarchical firewall policy,
we introduced precisely

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.960
to address this
type of pain-points.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.300
With hierarchical
firewall policies,

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.190
users can define and
attach firewall policies

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:09.610
at higher level
off the hierarchy,

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:11.890
specifically
organization and folder

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.130
nodes where the
centralized control can

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:16.720
be defined and enforced.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:23.820
Firewall policies is different
from a traditional firewall

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:24.710
rules.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:27.540
It is a container that can
contain multiple firewall

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:28.480
rules.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.550
In there it can be
defined once, and then

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:36.220
later attached to different
nodes in the hierarchy.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:39.370
Also, when you try to
modify the rule set

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.910
within a central
policy, you can actually

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.680
execute it in a batch mode.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.720
With firewall policies
attached to a different level

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.090
of the node in the
resource hierarchy,

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:58.050
they follow a very strict
enforcement sequence from top

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.310
to the bottom.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:03.540
All traffic must pass the
org level security rules

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.720
before they are being
evaluated at the folder level,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.610
and then eventually
at the VPC level.

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:13.290
This type of model made it
impossible for project owners

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.890
to bypass or overwrite
security policies set

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:18.530
by the central team.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:22.710
They also make sure that any new
project and new networks added

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.260
to the organization
can be protected

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.590
by those central
policies from day one.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.140
Defining rules at the
org and folder level

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.940
doesn't mean that
all those rules

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.860
must apply to all
instances in the same way.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.720
The user can actually use
target service account

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:46.040
to designate certain
instances for selected moves.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:50.000
For example, a user could
define a firewall rule

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.500
sitting at the org level that
forbids all the egress traffic

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.530
and only allows the egress
traffic for a selected

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.830
set of proxy servers
that with certain service

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.490
account associated with it.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:10.170
Another module that we recently
be brought to the public beta

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.600
is called Firewall Insights.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.900
This module is introduced
to help our customer manage

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:20.940
complex firewall rule sets
in a large VPC environment.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.430
Many enterprise
customers face challenges

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.850
in maintaining a clean
and robust firewall

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:29.730
rule set over time, and
face many challenges

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:33.530
such as massive volumes.

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:36.300
A VPC can have hundreds,
or even thousands

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.780
of rules associated with it.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.830
Overgranting is another issue.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:46.110
The lack of visibility into
the [INAUDIBLE] usage data

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.260
is typically very difficult
to find them out and eliminate

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.300
those type of risks.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.970
Human error is another
factor that could introduce

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.860
misconfingured firewall rules.

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:58.640
And outdated rules
were the ones that

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.180
left over by the
previous project

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:02.440
and never gets cleaned up.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:08.520
With Firewall Insights,
we introduce a set

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.520
of capabilities that hopefully
will help our customer better

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.620
manage those challenges.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.310
First, we introduce a set of
metrics for firewall usage,

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:22.130
such as the hit count
and last hit time.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:24.180
Using these metrics,
users can easily

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:28.410
find those allow rules that
are not used, and clean him up

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:31.700
in order to tighten
the security boundary.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:34.100
During troubleshooting
time, users also

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.860
can make use of those
rules to know precisely

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:40.732
which rules are being hit when
certain traffic pass through.

00:13:40.732 --> 00:13:43.850
It can also detect
abnormal traffic patterns

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:48.670
by observing sudden
jumps in their hit trend.

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:52.340
All those metrics
exported to Stackdriver

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:56.230
where a user could use them
to create custom dashboard,

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:58.930
define user found
alerts in order

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:03.470
to catch conditions
they are interested in.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:06.090
Firewall Insights
also brings a module

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:09.840
that automatically scans
the entire project for all

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.480
the firewall rules and
detects shadow relationships.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:16.440
A shadow rule is a rule
that is overshadowed

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:20.790
by one or more rules sitting
at a higher priority.

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:25.080
Without machines help, it's
typically very difficult

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:27.260
to find it out manually.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:33.840
With Firewall Insights we report
centrally for all the shadow

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.360
relationships in a project.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:40.230
And we refresh that periodically
so the user could simply

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:44.310
go through the report, examine
each shadow relationships,

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.170
and eliminate them as needed.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:51.580
Next, we're going to
talk about the new module

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:56.760
that we recently brought to
market called Packet Mirroring.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:00.410
So what is Packet Mirroring,
and why do you need it?

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:03.650
As we know that to build
a robust threat detection

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:07.250
program, we need the combination
of the network's threat

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:11.180
detection system
combined with EDRNC

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:13.760
to give us the critical
visibility that

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:17.360
enables quick, swift
investigation and response,

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:21.720
and then minimize the
damage during an attack.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.410
Network data is often
considered a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.190
It provides complete visibility
of the network activities.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:30.990
It's really to
difficult to evade,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.530
and cannot be easily turned off.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.740
During an attack
when an infected host

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:41.160
is trying to reach another
host, the moment a packet leaves

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.940
the host, it will leave the
trace in your network data

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.470
which will be captured.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:50.930
Therefore it's often considers
a very reliable data source

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:53.480
for threat investigation.

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.850
Many of my enterprise
customers are already

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:59.000
taking advantage of this
set of data new setup tools

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.210
to collect to and analyze
this data for their security

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.970
purposes.

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:09.440
When moving to the public
cloud, they sometimes cannot get

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:13.550
the same level of visibility
into their network traffic.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.850
However, when they are
moving more and more workload

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.790
into the cloud, their networks
start to become very complex.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.490
Therefore, they
demand the same level

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:26.620
of visibility for the network
traffic, for performance

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.880
monitoring, and security
control purposes.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.730
GCP Packet Mirroring
is introduced precisely

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:36.090
to address this type of need.

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.100
They help the user increase
their confidence in their cloud

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.280
migration and ensures
a smooth transition,

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.770
and gives them the
ability to implement

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:47.998
advanced and proactive
security contracts.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:53.600
GCP Packet Mirroring
is now generally

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:58.620
available in all regions
for all VM instance types.

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:02.900
It can help capture traffic
from all different directions.

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:06.210
Traffic between VM
to VM, between VM

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:11.640
to internet, and also between
VM to Google services.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.359
When combined with
intrusion detection systems,

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:18.300
it can help users easily detect
abnormal traffic patterns,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.990
and capture the emerging risk.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:25.500
It can also help them satisfy
the compliance requirement,

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.569
and also used for
application performance

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:31.840
monitoring and
troubleshooting purposes.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.800
Now let's take a
look how the Packet

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:38.790
Mirroring works under the hood.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:43.790
Packet Mirroring can be enabled
for both GCE-VM instances as

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.800
well as GKE clusters.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.120
It happens at the
instance level.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:52.200
So whenever the Packet
Mirroring is enabled,

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:56.130
it captures all the traffic,
both ingress and egress,

00:17:56.130 --> 00:18:00.290
including the header
and the payload.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:02.050
To configure a
Packet Mirroring, you

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.200
need both front-end
and back-end policies.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.120
The front-end policy will
define which instance will be

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.610
enabled for Packet Mirroring.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.640
You can specify a specific
individual instance

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.200
to be activated.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.630
You can also specify
a group of instances

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:20.450
by specifying the
subnet or using

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:26.370
network TACs The back-end policy
specifies where the collector

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:27.660
endpoint is.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:30.405
A collector endpoint is
an internal load balancer

00:18:30.405 --> 00:18:35.170
that sits in front of a
group of collector VMs.

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:37.400
The collector VMs
usually are running

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:42.940
a set of partner solutions, such
as the Parardos IDS systems.

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:46.800
When the policy is defined,
the Packet Mirroring traffic

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.830
will be piped through the
internal load balancer

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:54.540
into these collector VMs where
the different systems will

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.480
consume those traffic to
perform different type

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:00.160
of functionalities.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:02.840
We recommend that you
use instant templates,

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:07.550
and manage instance group to
manage these collector VMs.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:11.240
It will give you auto-scaling
and auto-healing capability,

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:15.140
and help you meet traffic
demand, and availability

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.510
requirements.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.390
Packet Mirroring can be
configured in your API,

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:21.830
and G Cloud command.

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.890
It can also be triggered and
enabled through the [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:27.440
functions.

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.750
While Packet Mirroring
provides critical data,

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:34.110
it enables a set of
critical use cases.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:35.720
It won't be the
complete solution

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:39.100
without our ecosystem
partner's support.

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:41.300
Therefore, we have
built an ecosystem

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:45.200
with vendors coming from network
security and network monitoring

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:49.580
domains so that our customers
could make use of the tool

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:53.300
that they are familiar with to
consume those Packet Mirroring

00:19:53.300 --> 00:20:00.910
traffic and build a solution
to satisfy their needs.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:04.540
To learn more about the
network security functionality

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:07.570
that we just went
over in this session,

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:11.890
here are a list of resources,
including blogs, documentation,

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:15.360
a live demo, and some
other next sections

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.310
that you'll want to refer to.

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:20.770
Thank you very much for
joining this session today.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:23.760
We hope you enjoy
the rest of the show.

